  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy.$",
  "worked_solution": "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{y}^{2}[x]_{0}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{y}^{2}(y-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}y\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{y}^{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
